To create the Snake sprite objects with a separate head and body in Unity 2D, set up individual 2D sprite game objects, assign distinct colors or textures, and organize them into prefabs.

1. Create the Snake Head Sprite

• Right-click in the Hierarchy window and select 2D Object > Sprites > Square (or import a custom pixel/art sprite).
• Name the GameObject SnakeHead.
• In the Sprite Renderer component, choose a bright or distinct color (such as bright yellow or vibrant green).
• Reset the Transform position to (0, 0, 0) using the Transform menu.

2. Create the Snake Body / Tail Sprite

• Right-click in the Hierarchy and select 2D Object > Sprites > Square.
• Name the GameObject SnakeBody (or SnakeTail).
• In the Sprite Renderer component, assign a complementary color (such as a darker green or greeny-yellow) so it stands out from the head.
• Scale the body sprite slightly down (e.g., set X and Y scale to 0.9 or adjust the collider size to 0.75) to prevent overlapping visual clutter or accidental self-collisions.
• Reset its Transform position as needed.

3. Configure Colliders and Rigidbody (Optional for Physics)

• Select SnakeHead, add a Rigidbody 2D component, and set Body Type to Kinematic (if moving via script).
• Add a Box Collider 2D to both the head and body, and check the Is Trigger box to handle eating and segment logic cleanly.

4. Turn Them into Prefabs

• Create a folder named Prefabs in your Project assets window.
• Drag both the SnakeHead and SnakeBody game objects from the Hierarchy into the Prefabs folder so you can instantiate and grow the body dynamically through C# or visual scripting.
• Delete the temporary SnakeBody instance from the scene once the prefab is saved.
